{% from 'networkpolicy.yml.j2' import networkpolicy with context %}
---
# NOTE: this role's OTHER policy file,
# networkpolicy-flaresolverr.yaml.j2, fences flaresolverr and is unrelated to this one. Both are
# applied.
#
# This rule is PORT-SCOPED, and for the callers below that scoping is the whole grant: the baseline
# admits none of monitor-bridge, sonarr, radarr or prowlarr-verify, so they reach prowlarr only
# through this rule and only on this one port.
#
# TRAEFIK IS IN THE CALLER LIST DELIBERATELY — do not remove it as redundant — but be accurate about why.
# The baseline admits traefik to every baseline-labelled pod on ANY port, and policies are
# additive, so while prowlarr carries that label the union is the same with or without this entry.
# The entry matters for a pod that lacks `netpol-baseline: enforced`, which this policy alone
# selects: without it the UI would 502 for that pod's lifetime. It also makes this policy state
# its own complete caller set rather than depending on the baseline continuing to admit traefik
# on all ports: if that baseline rule is ever port-scoped, or this pod template loses the label,
# this line is what keeps the UI up.
#
# sonarr and radarr reach prowlarr by bare name from their own application databases — the indexer
# definitions live on their PVCs, not in this repo.
#
# prowlarr-verify is the CONTROL leg of this role's OWN isolation check, the Job in
# templates/verify/ that Flux runs in the `prowlarr-verify` Kustomization: before it asserts
# flaresolverr is unreachable from outside prowlarr, it first proves its own pod networking works
# at all by dialling prowlarr on this port. Fence that leg out and the control can never pass,
# so the Job fails on every revision — a negative test with no working control proves nothing.
# Same shape as k8s/roles/registry/templates/networkpolicy.yaml.j2 admitting its own
# registry-selftest-push/registry-selftest-pull Jobs: a test pod that must reach the workload it
# is fencing. Do not "clean this up" as an unused caller. It must never join flaresolverr's fence,
# which the same Job proves refuses it.
#
# The caller list is `netpol_from:` on this role's containers_list entry
# (cluster/services.yml), each caller's reason beside it. The entry's
# `netpol_role_owned: true` keeps netpol-baseline from rendering a second copy.
{{ networkpolicy(container_item.name, container_item.port, container_item | netpol_callers) }}
